{
  "narrative_body": "Finland's two governing coalition parties both launched their 2027 election campaigns this week — simultaneously, with rival branding and competing promises — while the prime minister was caught misrepresenting employment figures in parliament for the third time this year.\n\nThe most striking element came from Kokoomus, which released a 92-page draft programme called \"Vapaus onnistua\" (\"Freedom to Succeed\"). Among proposals for university fees, Alko monopoly dissolution, and a debt brake, the document included something that has never appeared in a Finnish governing party's programme: a stated readiness to \"assess without preconceptions\" the future of Åland's demilitarization. The islands have been demilitarized under international treaty since 1856, a status reaffirmed in 1921 and 1947. Post-NATO accession, the question of whether that status still serves Finnish security has circulated among analysts; it has now entered governing-party electoral positioning. Petteri Orpo, the prime minister, also promised to reveal Kokoomus's fiscal consolidation plan — €8-11 billion over the next term — by autumn 2026.\n\nRiikka Purra, the finance minister and Finns Party leader, ran her own event the same week. Billing it \"Kiihdytysajot\" (\"Acceleration Races\"), she unveiled a new dandelion party logo — described as \"brave and determined, yet humble\" — and a new slogan, \"Kotimaa kutsuu\" (\"Motherland Calls\"). She pledged no further welfare cuts this term, called for constitutional amendments to restrict immigration, and demanded future coalition partners commit to strict immigration conditions as a precondition of any deal. She attacked SDP leader Antti Lindtman at length, accusing him of \"prime minister disease.\" The SDP hit back, with its deputy chair listing labor reform cuts to workers and accusing Ms Purra of amnesia about her own government's record. The Finns Party has fallen from roughly 20% support in 2023 to around 14% in current polls; the new branding and hardened immigration line are a direct attempt to recover ground by re-separating the party from Kokoomus on economic policy.\n\nThe dual launch confirms what has been building for weeks: both coalition partners are now campaigning as much as governing, competing for their own 2027 shares while keeping the parliamentary majority intact to complete the term.\n\nMr Orpo's position was not helped by a dispute in parliament over employment data. He claimed during the public finance framework debate that employment was \"almost the same\" as when his government took office. Statistics Finland data shows it was 64,000 lower in March 2026 than in June 2023, seasonally adjusted. When pressed, Mr Orpo acknowledged the claim was \"open to interpretation\" and cited a distinction between the employment rate and the employment count. Yle, Helsingin Sanomat, and Ilta-Sanomat all independently confirmed the factual error. It was the third such documented misrepresentation of employment data in parliament this year.\n\nOn security, the week brought confirmation of Finland's dual-track approach to Ukraine. Antti Häkkänen, the defence minister, met his Ukrainian counterpart Mykhailo Fedorov and confirmed that joint drone production is accelerating — covering development, components, counter-drone systems, command structures, and drone AI. In the same meeting, Mr Häkkänen told Mr Fedorov directly that Ukrainian drones transiting Finnish airspace \"must not happen\" and received an apology. The Finnish Army also unveiled the M26, a drone built with lessons drawn from Ukrainian battlefield experience — GNSS-independent navigation, jamming-resistant, 20-plus km range — worth roughly €15 million. The Defence Forces Logistics Command joined CORPUS, a Ukraine-originated defence procurement coalition whose founding members include Norway, Sweden, Italy, and the UK, as a founding member. Finland is deepening the production partnership and embedding it in multilateral architecture; it is also enforcing a hard sovereignty boundary.\n\nThree exercises — SILTA26, KETJU26, and Mighty Arrow 26 — ran simultaneously. The KETJU26 logistics exercise involved roughly 100 government bodies and private sector firms operating under a scenario in which military force was being used against Finland.\n\nThe Germany relationship moved in a different direction this week. When Frank-Walter Steinmeier, Germany's federal president, visited Helsinki on 7 May, his delegation unusually included Bundeswehr command and defence ministry leadership. Alexander Stubb, the president, named Finnish firms — Patria, Nokia, ICEYE — as contributors to German and European rearmament, and the pair toured a civilian bomb shelter, framing Finland's civil defence model as something Germany might adapt. Mr Stubb said he had \"no doubt the US would come to our aid\" but added separately that Europeans should assess whether American Russia policy serves European interests — a hedge he has maintained consistently across several appearances this week. Mr Orpo, for his part, told an Aalto University audience that \"values without power cannot be defended\" and announced Finland will host a European Arctic Summit in autumn 2026.",
  "other_stories": [
